Regions|An AWS Region is a geographic area containing multiple Availability Zones.|Resources and service endpoints are often regional, while some services have global components. Select the intended region explicitly in automation. Regional isolation, latency, residency and service availability influence architecture and recovery design.|aws configure get region
Availability Zones|An Availability Zone is an isolated infrastructure location within a Region.|Distributing workloads across AZs can tolerate some local failures when routing, data and capacity support failover. Multiple instances in one AZ do not provide AZ resilience. Cross-AZ designs also need to consider latency and transfer cost.|
IAM roles|An IAM role defines permissions that trusted principals can assume to obtain temporary credentials.|The trust policy controls who may assume the role; permission policies constrain what the resulting session can do. Role assumption is not the same as permission to access every resource. Prefer workload roles over embedded long-lived keys.|aws sts get-caller-identity
Least privilege|Least privilege grants only the actions and resources required for a defined task.|IAM evaluation combines applicable policies and explicit denies; organization and session controls can further constrain access. Start with the intended API and resource, then use conditions where appropriate. Avoid solving AccessDenied with broad administrator access.|
S3 storage|Amazon S3 stores objects identified by keys within buckets.|Objects are not ordinary mounted block-device files. Access policies, encryption, versioning and lifecycle rules govern different behaviors. Prefixes organize keys but are not traditional directories. Choose storage classes and lifecycle transitions around access and recovery needs.|aws s3api head-bucket --bucket BUCKET
EBS|Amazon EBS provides block volumes for compatible compute workloads within an Availability Zone.|A volume's filesystem and application consistency are separate from the block service. Snapshots support recovery and copying workflows, but application-aware procedures may be needed. Verify attachment, device identity and mount state before modifying data.|aws ec2 describe-volumes --volume-ids VOLUME_ID
Security groups|Security groups provide stateful traffic filtering for associated network interfaces.|Rules permit traffic; response traffic for tracked flows is handled statefully. They do not create routes or make an application listen. Evaluate source identities, ports and attached groups rather than assuming a subnet label determines access.|aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids GROUP_ID
VPC subnets|A VPC subnet is an IP range in one Availability Zone with associated routing and network controls.|A public route to an internet gateway alone does not make every resource public; addressing and security controls also matter. Private workloads may use NAT or service endpoints for egress. Avoid overlapping ranges across connected networks.|aws ec2 describe-subnets --subnet-ids SUBNET_ID
NAT gateways|A NAT gateway provides supported outbound address translation for private workloads.|A public NAT gateway needs appropriate routing and public-side connectivity. It does not accept arbitrary unsolicited inbound connections to private clients. Consider AZ failure behavior, connection limits and data-processing cost when designing egress.|
VPC endpoints|VPC endpoints provide private access paths to supported services without routing that traffic through public internet egress.|Gateway and interface endpoints have different routing, DNS and policy behavior. Endpoint policy is an additional control, not a replacement for service authorization. Verify DNS resolution and route selection from the workload.|
Application Load Balancers|An Application Load Balancer routes HTTP-family requests using listeners, rules and target groups.|Target health determines eligibility, while listener rules select destinations. A healthy check path may not exercise the failing business operation. Inspect target reasons, ports, security groups and application responses together.|aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn TARGET_GROUP_ARN
Auto Scaling|Auto Scaling changes group capacity according to configured policies and health replacement rules.|Desired, minimum and maximum capacity constrain behavior. Startup delay, health checks, quotas and available capacity affect how quickly new instances help. Scaling the frontend does not automatically increase a bottlenecked database's capacity.|
CloudWatch metrics|CloudWatch metrics are timestamped measurements identified by namespace, metric name and dimensions.|Statistics and periods determine how data is aggregated. Missing data and delayed reporting need explicit interpretation. Select the correct dimension set and time window before comparing values or changing alarm thresholds.|aws cloudwatch describe-alarms
CloudTrail|CloudTrail records supported account activity and API events for audit and investigation.|Events identify actors, actions, resources and timing, subject to configured event coverage and retention. Management and data events have different collection requirements. Lack of an event in one view does not prove no action occurred.|aws cloudtrail lookup-events --max-results 10
Shared responsibility|The shared-responsibility model divides security work between AWS and the customer.|AWS operates underlying cloud infrastructure; customer responsibilities vary with the service and include data, identities and configuration. Managed services reduce some operating tasks but do not make application authorization or data protection automatic.|
S3 versioning|S3 versioning retains multiple versions of objects in a bucket.|Overwriting or deleting a key may leave earlier versions recoverable, with delete markers affecting the current view. Lifecycle policies can remove versions, so recovery depends on retention and permissions. Versioning is useful protection but not a complete independent backup strategy.|aws s3api get-bucket-versioning --bucket BUCKET
RDS availability|RDS availability features provide managed database redundancy and failover options depending on engine and deployment type.|Multi-AZ configurations address availability, while read replicas commonly address read scaling or other recovery patterns. Client reconnect behavior, DNS and transaction retry semantics still matter during failover. Test the actual engine and topology.|
Read replicas|Read replicas serve replicated database data and can offload eligible read traffic.|Replication lag means reads may be stale. Route consistency-sensitive reads appropriately and monitor lag under write load. Promotion changes topology and application routing; it is not the same operation as every managed failover mechanism.|
Lambda retries|Lambda retry behavior depends on the invocation source and delivery mode.|Synchronous callers, asynchronous invocation and event-source mappings have different retry and failure handling. Duplicate processing is possible, so make handlers idempotent and configure failure destinations or queues as appropriate. Match timeout and concurrency settings to dependencies.|
SQS visibility|The SQS visibility timeout temporarily hides a received message while a consumer processes it.|A message becomes available again if it is not deleted before visibility expires. Choose a timeout aligned with processing and extend it when supported for long work. Consumers still need duplicate-safe behavior because delivery semantics can permit repeats.|aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url QUEUE_URL --attribute-names All
Dead-letter queues|A dead-letter queue isolates messages that repeatedly fail processing under a configured redrive policy.|It preserves evidence and prevents poison messages from blocking normal progress. Alarm on arrival and investigate before replay. Fix the cause, validate replay safety and control replay rate to avoid recreating the incident.|
DynamoDB keys|DynamoDB primary keys determine how items are identified and distributed for access.|Partition-key design should spread load while supporting required queries; a sort key organizes related items within a partition-key value. Hot keys and unsuitable access patterns cannot always be solved by increasing aggregate capacity.|
KMS controls|AWS KMS manages cryptographic keys and authorized cryptographic operations.|Key policies, IAM policies and grants interact with service usage. Permission to read an encrypted object does not necessarily include permission to decrypt it. Key deletion and disabled keys can make data inaccessible, so lifecycle controls are critical.|aws kms describe-key --key-id KEY_ID
Secrets rotation|Secret rotation changes credentials and updates consumers through a coordinated lifecycle.|The new credential must be valid at the target and available to clients before old access is retired. Cache lifetimes and long-lived connections can delay adoption. Test rollback and partial-failure behavior rather than assuming storage update alone completes rotation.|
Route 53 failover|DNS failover changes answers based on configured routing and health behavior.|Cached answers remain until their effective lifetime expires, and clients may cache independently. The destination needs enough capacity and current data. DNS steering does not move existing connections or guarantee application correctness.|
CloudFront caching|CloudFront caches eligible responses at edge locations according to cache policy and origin behavior.|The cache key determines which requests share a response. Omitting a necessary variation can serve incorrect content; excessive variation reduces hit rate. Distinguish cache TTL, invalidation and versioned object naming when deploying updates.|
Cross-account access|Cross-account access authorizes a principal in one AWS account to use resources in another.|Role trust or resource policies and the caller's permissions must align; explicit denies still apply. Use narrowly scoped trust and conditions appropriate to the relationship. Verify caller identity before diagnosing the target policy.|
Service quotas|Service quotas bound resource counts, request rates or other usage within defined scopes.|Some quotas are adjustable and others are fixed; scopes may be regional or account-specific. Monitor headroom before growth or failover. Retrying a quota failure aggressively usually adds noise rather than capacity.|
Cost attribution|Cost attribution assigns cloud spending to teams, products or environments.|Consistent accounts, tags and billing dimensions make allocation possible, subject to service support and activation behavior. Separate usage changes from price or discount effects. Optimize measured cost without sacrificing required reliability or recovery capacity.|
Disaster recovery|Disaster recovery restores workloads and data after failures beyond normal availability mechanisms.|Choose backup/restore, warm standby or other patterns from RTO and RPO. Include identity, encryption keys, networking and external dependencies. Test restoration in the intended recovery location; a copied snapshot alone is not a recovered application.|
